# --- s024 Book I d.3 q.9 = Ord. I d.3 p.3 q.2 (Vat. nn.401-553)
n("n432","s024-01","Nona questio tertie distinctionis","la","source","= Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 3 p. 3 q. 2 (ed. Vat. III, nn. 401–553): *utrum pars intellectiva proprie sumpta vel aliquid eius sit causa totalis gignens notitiam actualem vel ratio gignendi*. Gorris's paraphrase (*i. si intellectus est causa totalis…*) is his own. His count of the questions of d. 3 (q. 9 here = Vat. p. 3 q. 2) confirms that his exemplar had two questions (his qq. 7–8) between Vat. p. 3 q. 1 and q. 2.","certain")
n("n433","s024-01","memoria fecunda est parens noticie genite","la","source","Scotus's first principal argument (n. 401), from Aug. *De Trin.* IX and XIV 6–7 (memory as parent of the word). The *ad oppositum* in Scotus (n. 405) is Avicenna, *Metaph.* IX; Gorris replaces it with Aug. *De Trin.* IX (12.18, knowledge begotten by knower and known), which he quotes in ¶4.","certain")
n("n434","s024-02","Henrici quodlibeto. 4. q. 7","la","source","The first opinion (n. 407), the whole activity attributed to the soul, imputed by Henry to Augustine. Gorris's references are Henry of Ghent, *Quodl.* IV q. 7 and V q. 5 (*pro ista opinione arguitur*, n. 409). The Vatican apparatus cites Henry, *Quodl.* V q. 14 and XIII q. 8 among others. I have not checked Gorris's question numbers against the Henry edition (Leuven Opera omnia).","to-verify")
n("n435","s024-02","Godofredi de fontibus","la","source","The second opinion (n. 422: *Alia est opinio, quae totaliter est in alio extremo*), the whole activity attributed to the object or phantasm: Godfrey of Fontaines. The Vatican editors cite *Quodl.* VI q. 15, VIII q. 2, IX q. 19 and XIII q. 3. Gorris's 'VI q. 7' may be a slip for q. 15; his other three references agree.","to-verify")
n("n436","s024-02","Henrici quodlibeto. 5. q. 14","la","source","The third opinion (n. 450): passive with respect to the first, simple and confused knowledge, active in discursive and distinct knowledge. Henry, *Quodl.* V q. 14. The fourth (n. 451, the 'retracting' position, *de passione respectu primae notitiae…*) Gorris assigns to *Quodl.* III q. 6. On the Vatican reading, n. 451 says *Iste idem doctor istam positionem… retractat*, and both opinions are Henry's.","to-verify")
n("n437","s024-03","Thome prima parte summe. q. 85. articulo. 2","la","source","The fifth opinion (n. 456), quoted almost verbatim by Gorris: Thomas Aquinas, *ST* I q. 85 a. 2 (the species as *id quo*), with q. 79 a. 2. The Vatican editors identify it rather as the common Thomist view (also Godfrey). Gorris's attribution to Thomas is traditional.","certain")
n("n438","s024-03","informatus illa specie","la","variant","A *ibi informatus* agrees with the *Ordinatio* (n. 456: *tamquam materiale ibi, informatus illa specie*). B reads *non informatus*, which contradicts the opinion reported and is an error of B.","certain")
n("n439","s024-03","Egidii quodlibeto. 3°. q. 12. 13. 14","la","source","The sixth opinion (n. 460: *quae redit in idem*): the actual knowledge begotten is itself the species. Gorris names Giles of Rome, *Quodl.* III qq. 12–14. Elsewhere the view is associated with Godfrey and Henry's critics. Not checked.","to-verify")
n("n440","s024-03","4°. quodlibeto Henrici. q. 8","la","source","The harmonization (*concordantur*) of the third and fourth opinions, n. 529. Its author, Scotus's *ille medius* (n. 531), is in fact an anonymous reconciler; the Vatican editors point to texts of Henry for the doctrine of the double *occursus* (*Quodl.* IV q. 8). Gorris attributes the source of the harmonization to Henry.","to-verify")
n("n441","s024-03","Hoc confirmatur secundum opinionem illius doctoris","la","textual","This lemma is not found in the Vatican text of p. 3 q. 2. It may belong to an additional passage present in Gorris's exemplar (one of the bracketed *$a…a$* additions in the Vatican edition, such as n. 454 or n. 524) or to another redaction.","to-verify")
n("n442","s024-03","quodlibeto. 5. q. 19","la","variant","B: *quolib. iiij. q. xix.* A: *quodlibeto. 5.* Henry's *Quodl.* IV has fewer than 19 questions on most counts, while *Quodl.* V has 40, so A's reading is preferable. The reference has not been verified.","uncertain")
n("n443","s024-03","post ration⟨ibus⟩","la","textual","Stain at the top right of p. 40. Supplied from B (*Ceterum respondendo rationibus thome in illo loco*). B omits *post*. The reply to Thomas's arguments for the fifth opinion is *Ord.* nn. 537–545.","uncertain")
n("n444","s024-03","intellectu⟨s⟩","la","textual","Stain at the top right of p. 40. Supplied from B (*intellect⁹*).","certain")
n("n445","s024-03","quam necessariam pertractant aliqui","la","variant","*Ord.* n. 541: *quam tamen auctoritatem in tantum pertractant aliqui, ut dicant intellectum possibilem esse in pura potentia… sicut materia*. Gorris (and B: *quã ncãriã*) read *necessariam* for *in tantum*. This is probably a misreading of an abbreviation in the common exemplar. The authority is Arist. *De an.* III 4, 429a24 (*nihil est actu eorum quae sunt ante intelligere*). Thomas, *ST* I q. 87 a. 1 and q. 79 a. 2, is correctly identified.","certain")
n("n446","s024-04","Sed veniendo ad opinionem Scoti","la","source","= *Ord.* nn. 486–488, closely paraphrased (*intellectio actualis est aliquid in nobis non perpetuum sed habens esse post non esse*; neither object nor soul the total cause). Scotus's positive account of partial causes follows at nn. 494–500.","certain")
n("n447","s024-05","nominales volunt concludere quod non requiritur species","la","commentary","Notable: Gorris turns the Augustinian dictum against the *nominales*, who deny the intelligible species (William of Ockham, *Rep.* II qq. 12–13; Gregory of Rimini, *In II Sent.* d. 7 q. 2). The argument from *Eth.* II, that there are only three things in the soul, is Ockham's (*Rep.* II q. 12–13, *Quodl.* IV q. 1 is the locus for parsimony). This is a second explicit polemic against the *via moderna* (cf. s020 *huius intentionis sunt nominales*).","to-verify")
n("n448","s024-05","tria sunt in anima. affectus potentia et habitus","la","source","Arist. *Eth. Nic.* II 4, 1105b19–21: *quae fiunt in anima tria sunt: passiones, potentiae, habitus*. Gorris's reply, that Aristotle is listing principles of *action* (in the ethical sense), is apt: the passage introduces the genus of virtue.","certain")
n("n449","s024-06","philosophus intelligit de potentiis anime quibus sumus formaliter et in actu secundo","la","source","*Ord.* n. 537, quoted nearly verbatim (*Philosophus locutus est communiter de potentiis animae, in quantum sunt quibus sumus formaliter in actu secundo…*).","certain")
n("n450","s024-06","Aristoteles non dixit quod intellectus in intelligendo non agit. ergo non agit","la","textual","The sentence as printed (also in B) contains a double negation that spoils the logic. The fallacy denounced is surely 'Aristotle did not say that the intellect acts in understanding; therefore it does not act'. The second *non* is either an error or should be read *dixit… agit*. The point corresponds to *Ord.* nn. 541–542.","uncertain")
n("n451","s024-06","quolibeto. 15","la","source","Scotus, *Quodl.* q. 15, on the active role of the intellect (*utrum intellectus agens sit causa activa…*). Scotus there defends the activity of the possible intellect in producing intellection.","certain")
n("n452","s024-07","quidam dicit sic et bene","la","commentary","Another anonymous 'quidam' (cf. s023 n429): a Scotist who distinguishes the passive reception of the species from the active exit into the act. This is close to Antonius Andreas and Francis of Meyronnes. Not identified.","to-verify")
n("n453","s024-07","actio est in agente. 3°. phisicorum","la","source","The principle that immanent action remains in the agent is *Metaph.* IX 8, 1050a30–b2. *Phys.* III 3, 202a13–b22 in fact places motion in the mobile. The citation of *Phys.* III is loose.","certain")
n("n454","s024-08","sol facit in hominem","la","source","Cf. Arist. *Phys.* II 2, 194b13 (*homo hominem generat et sol*). The principle *agens est praestantius patiente* is Aug. *De Gen. ad litt.* XII 16.33, discussed at *Ord.* n. 507 (*non est immediate vera*). For *viventia non viventibus meliora*, cf. Aug. *De Trin.* XI 5.8 and *De lib. arb.* II 3.7; Scotus quotes the dictum at n. 409.","certain")
n("n455","s024-08","memoria fecunda includit potentiam intellectiuam cum obiecto","la","source","= *Ord.* n. 547 sense: memory as *parens* includes the object present in the intelligible species (cf. d. 2 on *memoria perfecta*).","certain")

# --- B collation (full): B36L–B38L
n("n1102","s024-04","Et sic quod obiectum","la","textual","B: *Et sic habetur quod obiectum*, supplying the verb that A's elliptical phrase implies; the English sense is unaffected.","certain")
upd("n452", append=" B reads *quidem dicit sic et bene* here too (cf. n429).")
upd("n454", append=" B: *sol facit hominem*, without *in*.")
